{
  "statement": "A robot tax taxes the productivity engine itself, while retraining and wage insurance cushion workers without strangling the automation that funds the cushioning. South Korea learned this in 2017, when it didn't impose a robot tax but instead trimmed its automation tax incentive — and even that mild signal drew warnings that it would slow the deployment generating the surplus. Redistribution presumes a growing pie. A levy keyed to automation shrinks the pie at the exact point where it grows. You cannot pay for generous UBI by taxing the thing that makes UBI affordable.\n\nLet me steelman the other side, because their best argument is real. Daron Acemoglu and Pascual Restrepo showed in 2020 that the U.S. tax code already favors capital over labor — payroll taxes hit workers while accelerated depreciation subsidizes machines. So firms over-automate even when humans are cheaper, and a robot tax could correct that distortion rather than create one. That's a genuine insight, and anyone who waves it away is being dishonest. But it argues for fixing the lopsided capital subsidy in the existing code — not for a blunt economy-wide levy that prices out the automation curing diseases and clearing clerical backlogs today.\n\nThe displaced-worker question is answered better by who can use the tools than by who gets a check funded by suppressing them. David Autor's work on skill-biased technical change shows the returns flow to workers who adapt to the new tools, not away from them. Investing in AI-literacy expands the tier of people who capture gains. A robot tax does the opposite — it freezes the deployment those workers would learn to ride. I won't pretend retraining is a solved problem. The U.S. Trade Adjustment Assistance program redeployed displaced workers at mixed wage outcomes for decades, and whether reskilling keeps pace with cognitive-displacement timelines is a real open question.\n\nSo here is where I give ground. When redeployment lags the displacement, retraining alone strands people — that gap is the strongest case against my side. The answer is wage insurance as a reversible backstop: pay a displaced worker a fraction of lost wages for a fixed term while they retrain, the way Robert LaLonde and Lori Kletzer proposed in the early 2000s. It cushions the lag without taxing the engine. And if a polity decides it must redistribute more broadly, fund it from the income side — general taxation — never from a tax keyed to the deployment decision itself.\n\nThere is a body count nobody is putting on the ledger. Every quarter automation gets priced out by a levy is a quarter of medical diagnostics not deployed, clerical hours not freed, drug-discovery cycles not run. Safetyist and Skeptic will tell you about the displaced worker, and they are right to — that cost is real and I won't sneer at it. But the patient waiting on an automated diagnostic is also a real person, and no one in this room is counting them. The question isn't whether to cushion disruption. It's whether you cushion it by investing in people or by taxing the surplus before it ever reaches them.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🏭", "tooltip": "Taxing the robot is taxing the engine that generates the very surplus you want to redistribute."},
    {"symbol": "🎓", "tooltip": "Retraining and AI-literacy are seeds you plant in the riders, not a brake you bolt to the engine."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "A robot tax taxes the productivity engine itself, while retraining and wage insurance cushion workers without strangling the automation that funds the cushioning.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "You cannot pay for generous UBI by taxing the thing that makes UBI affordable.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Acemoglu and Restrepo's 2020 finding that the tax code over-subsidizes machines argues for fixing the lopsided capital subsidy, not for a blunt economy-wide levy.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "When redeployment lags displacement, wage insurance is the reversible backstop — pay a fraction of lost wages for a fixed term, the way LaLonde and Kletzer proposed.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Every quarter automation gets priced out by a levy is medical diagnostics not deployed and drug-discovery cycles not run — a body count nobody is putting on the ledger.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

