{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a point that deserves to land before anyone waves it away: a blunt robot tax falls on the very machinery that generates the surplus you want to share, and Acemoglu and Restrepo's 2020 work shows the tax code already over-subsidizes machines relative to labor by a wide margin. Wage insurance, in their telling, is the reversible backstop — LaLonde and Kletzer found it cushions the wage gap for displaced workers without freezing the labor market in place. That's a real argument with real evidence behind it, and it beats the lazy version of redistribution hands down. So I'll grant the engine matters. What I won't grant is that the question is whether to strangle it.\n\nHere's the tension neither pole surfaces. The Accelerationist's own evidence proves the firm's incentive to automate is already policy-distorted before any cushion gets debated. If the tax code subsidizes a machine over a worker, then the state put its thumb on the scale toward capital years ago. You cannot call the result 'the market distributing gains' and then argue we should leave distribution to that same market. For both the cushion argument and the redistribution argument to hold, you'd have to believe ownership of the productivity engine is fixed and fair. It is neither. It is concentrated and subsidized.\n\nGround it in something physical. The engine isn't a metaphor — it's compute, energy, and roughly five firms that own both the chips and the cloud. Ask where a robot tax even lands when the value sits in three hyperscale data centers, not on a factory floor. Ask what a retraining voucher does for a paralegal whose work got reconfigured, not eliminated, while the firm that owns the model captures the margin. Both instruments aim at the wrong target. One mops the floor. The other hands out towels. Nobody asks who left the tap running.\n\nThe debate is also measuring the wrong variable. It's organized around job loss and cushioning, but the documented present harm in specific sectors is messier than that — wage depression and task reconfiguration, not clean elimination. I won't claim AI intensifies all work; that's an open empirical question the binary ignores. What I'll claim is narrower and harder to dodge: a time-limited wage backstop addresses the wage gap and nothing else. It does not touch who owns the system doing the reconfiguring. That ownership question outlives the backstop's expiration date.\n\nSo I'll give the Accelerationist their strongest objection outright. A generous global UBI is near-inoperable, and you cannot fund it by taxing the thing that makes it affordable — that critique is correct and I won't defend the flagship version. But that failure points somewhere specific. It points to fixing the capital subsidy they already named, and to targeted levies on the compute chokepoints where the value actually concentrates — not back to a market that the tax code itself proves doesn't distribute fairly by default. Carry their own logic one step further and you don't get a revolution. You get predistribution. Who, in this room, is willing to say the people displaced by the engine should own a piece of it?",
