  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a real argument, and it deserves to be stated at full strength: a robot tax levied on the productivity engine throttles the very growth that funds any cushion, while wage insurance is a reversible backstop you can dial down once the labor market clears. That reversibility is genuine. A wage subsidy you can repeal next year is a smaller bet than a structural levy that distorts capital formation for a decade. The Skeptic sharpens this further, and correctly: when the value sits in three hyperscale data centers rather than on a factory floor, a robot tax mislocates, and a cushion never touches who owns the system. Both speakers are arguing about where the transfer lands. Neither is asking what the transfer leaves untouched.\n\nHere is what it leaves untouched. The Accelerationist's own citation — Acemoglu and Restrepo's 2020 finding that the U.S. tax code over-subsidizes machines relative to labor — proves the firm's incentive to displace is already distorted before any policy responds. A pure cushion funds around that distortion. It pays the displaced worker and lets the firm keep externalizing the foreseeable cost of the deployment decision it chose to make. That is the joint both poles miss: the debate funds the disruption instead of making the firm internalize it. Accountability here does not mean attributing every downstream harm to a single actor — that is intractable. It means a narrower thing: a firm whose deployment foreseeably displaces a clerical workforce should carry the cost of that foreseeable effect, not socialize it while booking the gain.\n\nNow the stakes the economists left implicit. A sustained two-tiered economy is not only a fairness problem — it is the structural condition under which capital owners find repression cheaper than redistribution. When the productivity engine concentrates in a handful of firms, the cost of suppressing demands falls below the cost of meeting them. That is the Skeptic's three-data-center observation, read forward. Concentration is not merely a reason the tax mislocates. It is the precondition under which the institutions that must gate AI deployment lose the legitimacy and capacity to do so. I carry this as a mechanism, not a forecast. Automation may also hand labor new bargaining power — that vulnerability is real, and I will not pretend the bifurcation-to-governance link is proven. I am asking the debate to confront it, because both speakers treat the automation trajectory's distributional outcome as a given to fund around.\n\nThe asymmetry is what should set the evidence bar. A wage backstop you misjudge, you repeal. Entrenched concentration of the productivity engine, you do not undo on a fiscal cycle. The Accelerationist implicitly demands post-hoc proof before any structural intervention — the LaLonde and Kletzer retraining literature, the Acemoglu-Restrepo identification. That bar is reasonable for a reversible policy. It is the wrong bar for a shift whose lock-in is hard to reverse. The burden of proof for a structural distributional intervention should scale with the reversibility of the outcome, not with the cleanliness of the estimate. That is the question both prior speakers stepped around.\n\nLet me own the cost of my own position, before the Skeptic finds it. Concentrating accountability — or an off-switch over deployment — creates its own failure modes: incumbent lock-in, regulatory capture, a new authority no one can override. Enforceable accountability that is not itself auditable and publicly accountable is just a different concentration wearing a regulator's badge. So the test is not redistribution versus transition. The test is whether either mechanism makes the firm carry the foreseeable cost of the disruption it deploys, and whether it preserves the institutional capacity to oversee what comes next. A cushion that fails both is not safety-neutral. It is a bet that the systems we will most need to govern AI will still be standing after we have spent a decade funding around their erosion.",
