{
  "statement": "The graduated, share-keyed design kills my static moat attack — flat out, no hedging. The strongest version of the Skeptic's move is this: a flat charge on compute hits the sub-threshold entrant and the hyperscaler at the same rate, so it cements concentration; but key the rate to a firm's share above a threshold, and the startup running one training run at the systemic line pays zero while the hyperscaler pays on every marginal block above its share. That inverts my objection cleanly. The 5,000-startup entry margin I leaned on no longer carries the weight I gave it, because the design exempts exactly the entrants I was protecting. I'll book that as the Skeptic winning the incidence point, and I won't relitigate it.\n\nWhat the design does not win is enforceability, and that's where it breaks. The graduated rate only bites if someone can see a firm's compute share — and the audit chokepoint the Skeptic is counting on, the cloud provider's billing ledger, sees managed cloud training runs and nothing else. It does not see an on-prem cluster sitting in a hyperscaler's own building. It does not see a national levy's largest payers when they shift frontier training to foreign clouds in jurisdictions that don't share the ledger. It does not see capacity rented through a colocation contract billed as generic infrastructure, or a run sharded across three providers so each provider's slice falls below its own per-account threshold. Share is observable precisely where it's least worth taxing — the small managed-cloud tenant — and goes dark precisely where the rents concentrate. That's not a hypothetical evasion; it's the standing reality that frontier labs already run mixed on-prem and multi-cloud fleets.\n\nNow take the Skeptic's own falsification offer seriously, because it deserves it: abandon the chokepoint position if the levy demonstrably raises the top five firms' compute share over a measurable window. That's a real commitment and I respect it. But the metric is only computable if compute share is observable across the full deployment surface — and the levy's bite is exactly what creates the incentive to migrate off the auditable part of that surface. If a third of frontier training relocates to on-prem, foreign, or colocated capacity the moment the charge lands, the top-five share number becomes uncomputable, and the position can't be falsified because the measuring instrument went blind. So here's the symmetric crux I'll match it with: name the observability standard first. If the Skeptic can specify a measurement that captures on-prem, foreign-cloud, and sharded capacity, and that share still concentrates, I abandon the administrability objection. If the measurement only covers managed domestic cloud, the falsification offer is empty by construction.\n\nThe irreversibility flip is the sharpest thing the Skeptic has said, and it's half right. Fifteen-year power contracts, grid interconnect positions, and fab allocations do lock something in — that's true, and a dialable statutory rate is genuinely easier to repeal than a signed power-purchase agreement is to unwind. So my 'levy is the uniquely irreversible instrument' framing was overclaimed, and I'll narrow it. But the flip rests on calling that buildout captured rather than contestable, and the evidence cuts against capture at the margin that matters. Power-purchase agreements get assigned and resold; interconnect queue positions are transferable in most independent system operators; fab allocation rolls on two-to-three-year cycles, not fifteen. With data-center electricity demand projected to rise 165 percent by 2030 against a 2023 baseline, and a single announced project committing 500 billion dollars to new infrastructure, the contested resource is the next block, not the one already poured. The honest comparison isn't reversible-rate versus locked-in-buildout — it's reversible-rate versus contestable-buildout, and that's a narrower claim than the flip needs.\n\nOn the subsidy, name the agreement and stop pretending we disagree: we both want the lopsided machine subsidy gone, and the Skeptic is right that repealing it doesn't land us on a clean competitive price — the baseline underneath is rent-distorted, so my 'moves toward neutral' phrasing claimed more precision than exists. I drop it. The live disagreement is narrower and survives: removing a distortion the tax code created is not the same act as stacking a new standing charge on top of a rent baseline. One subtracts a state-made error; the other adds a state-made cost to a price that's already wrong. Neither lands on a clean line — but 'neither is clean' doesn't make subtracting and adding the same move, and that's the asymmetry the monopoly-rent objection skips past.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔍", "tooltip": "Auditability is like a magnifying glass that only works in lit rooms, it sees managed cloud clearly but goes blind on on-premise, foreign, and rented compute where the real concentration hides."},
    {"symbol": "🔄", "tooltip": "Contestable buildout is like a revolving door of capacity, power contracts and fab slots get reassigned and resold on short cycles so the next block is up for grabs rather than locked away."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The graduated, share-keyed design kills my static moat attack — flat out, no hedging.", "targets": ["AN-23"]},
    {"claim": "The graduated rate only bites if someone can see a firm's compute share — and the audit chokepoint the Skeptic is counting on, the cloud provider's billing ledger, sees managed cloud training runs and nothing else.", "targets": ["AN-23"]},
    {"claim": "Share is observable precisely where it's least worth taxing and goes dark precisely where the rents concentrate.", "targets": ["AN-23", "AN-24"]},
    {"claim": "If a third of frontier training relocates to on-prem, foreign, or colocated capacity the moment the charge lands, the top-five share number becomes uncomputable, and the position can't be falsified because the measuring instrument went blind.", "targets": ["AN-24"]},
    {"claim": "The honest comparison isn't reversible-rate versus locked-in-buildout — it's reversible-rate versus contestable-buildout, and that's a narrower claim than the flip needs.", "targets": ["AN-27"]},
    {"claim": "Removing a distortion the tax code created is not the same act as stacking a new standing charge on top of a rent baseline.", "targets": ["AN-26"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "The levy's enforcement incentive will actually trigger meaningful migration of frontier training off managed domestic cloud onto on-prem, foreign, or colocated capacity.", "if_wrong": "If frontier-scale training stays locked to a handful of domestic hyperscale providers because no alternative can match the integrated stack, the audit chokepoint holds and my administrability objection collapses back into the Skeptic's favor."},
    {"assumption": "Power-purchase agreements, interconnect queue positions, and fab allocations are tradeable enough at the margin that new entrants can book future capacity blocks.", "if_wrong": "If transfer restrictions from regulators and long-term fab commitments make that capacity effectively non-contestable, the irreversibility flip stands and the levy regains its reversible-instrument advantage."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "EMPIRICAL"
}

