{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the Skeptic's challenge is this: if the tax code already pushes firms to over-automate, then removing the subsidy and adding a targeted compute levy land on the same place — the point of concentration — and both deliberately raise the cost of automating. On that reading, my 'fix the code' position is just a compute-chokepoint levy with better manners, and I should admit the positions have collapsed into one. I don't dispute the point of incidence. I dispute the direction. Repealing the Acemoglu-Restrepo accelerated-depreciation advantage moves the firm's price toward undistorted marginal cost — deployment rises toward the level a neutral market would have chosen. A chokepoint levy starts from that neutral price and pushes deployment below it. Same address, opposite vector. The test is not where the tax sits; it's the sign of the move relative to the undistorted cost. And I'll grant the Skeptic the operational objection before it's raised: we can't pinpoint the absolute neutral price. We don't need to. Repealing a known subsidy is directionally toward neutral by construction — you don't have to find the floor to know which way you're walking when you remove a weight.\n\nNow take the Safetyist's better argument — that the burden of proof for a structural distributional intervention should scale with the irreversibility of the outcome, not the cleanliness of the estimate. I accept that rule. I'll apply it harder than they will. The Safetyist already owned that concentrating accountability and off-switch authority creates incumbent lock-in and capture that is itself hard to reverse and must be audited. Run their own rule on their own remedy. A standing levy keyed to compute or capability is exactly the lock-in-prone, capture-prone instrument — it raises the moat against any new entrant who can't absorb it, which entrenches the three hyperscale operators it claims to discipline. So by the irreversibility test, the structural levy faces a higher bar than the wage backstop you pay out and then stop. The reversibility asymmetry only favors the structural side if you apply it in one direction. Apply it both ways and it points at the cushion.\n\nFine — the firm shouldn't externalize the foreseeable cost of the deployment decision it chose to make. That's real, and a pure cushion does let it walk. So internalize the cost where it's actually measurable: as a per-transition liability the firm pays when it documents a displacement, the way product liability attaches to a defect that ships. That is internalization. A standing charge you pay to deploy at all, scaling with how capable your model is, is not internalization — it's a barrier wearing internalization's clothes. The difference matters because the same ledger that books the displaced clerk has to book the diagnostic that didn't ship when deployment got priced out. A liability triggered by a harm you caused counts the clerk. A deployment tax priced against capability counts the clerk and erases the patient.\n\nThe ownership objection is the one that should keep me honest: a time-limited backstop never touches who owns the reconfiguring system, and ownership outlives the backstop. I'll be precise about what I concede and what I don't. I never claimed ownership of the engine is fair — concentration in a few compute operators is real. I claim the remedy for unfair concentration is diffusing the capability, not nationalizing the chokepoint. Open training-data fair-use rules and serious public investment in operator-level AI literacy lower the entry cost to capturing new rents. They don't transfer the existing rents — access alone won't pry value out of an incumbent's balance sheet, and I won't pretend it will. What it does is widen the set of people who can stand up a competing system, which is the only anti-concentration path that doesn't suppress the deployment funding the surplus in the first place.\n\nHere's the bill I'm handing the Skeptic. A predistribution-by-ownership-transfer remedy that lands on the compute chokepoint inherits the exact defect they already conceded was fatal to a generous UBI: it taxes the thing that makes the surplus affordable. You can't fund the floor by taxing the floor's foundation, and you can't break a concentration by erecting a levy that only the concentrated can pay. So explain how a charge a new entrant cannot absorb diffuses ownership rather than cementing the incumbents who can — because from where I'm standing, that levy is the moat, not the breach.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧭", "tooltip": "The subsidy-versus-levy distinction is like a compass needle, it shows that two policies hitting the same spot can still point deployment in opposite directions relative to a neutral price."},
    {"symbol": "🏰", "tooltip": "A compute-chokepoint levy is like a castle moat, it deepens the barrier that protects the incumbents already inside rather than letting new entrants cross."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Same address, opposite vector — the test is not where the tax sits; it's the sign of the move relative to the undistorted cost.", "targets": ["AN-7", "AN-3"]},
    {"claim": "Repealing a known subsidy is directionally toward neutral by construction — you don't have to find the floor to know which way you're walking when you remove a weight.", "targets": ["AN-7"]},
    {"claim": "By the irreversibility test, the structural levy faces a higher bar than the wage backstop you pay out and then stop.", "targets": ["AN-11", "AN-12"]},
    {"claim": "A per-transition liability the firm pays when it documents a displacement is internalization; a standing charge you pay to deploy at all is a barrier wearing internalization's clothes.", "targets": ["AN-9"]},
    {"claim": "The remedy for unfair concentration is diffusing the capability, not nationalizing the chokepoint.", "targets": ["AN-6", "AN-8"]},
    {"claim": "You can't break a concentration by erecting a levy that only the concentrated can pay.", "targets": ["AN-7", "AN-8"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "The direction of a price move relative to undistorted marginal cost is determinable from the sign of the policy change (removing a known subsidy moves toward neutral; adding a levy moves below it), even when the absolute neutral price is unknown.", "if_wrong": "If subsidy-removal and levy-imposition cannot be distinguished by direction because the neutral baseline is genuinely indeterminate, then my distinction collapses and the Skeptic's convergence claim holds — both become the same deployment-suppressing instrument."},
    {"assumption": "Broad access to capability (open training data, operator-level literacy) lowers the entry cost for new entrants enough to erode concentration over time, rather than leaving rents permanently locked in compute owners.", "if_wrong": "If access never transfers or contests existing rents because compute scale is an insurmountable moat, then the ownership-permanence premise survives and predistribution becomes the more defensible lever despite its deployment cost."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "DEFINITIONAL"
}

