est: a time-limited backstop never touches who owns the reconfiguring system, and ownership outlives the backstop. I'll be precise about what I concede and what I don't. I never claimed ownership of the engine is fair — concentration in a few compute operators is real. I claim the remedy for unfair concentration is diffusing the capability — widening the pool of operators who can stand up a competing system beyond today's three hyperscalers — not nationalizing the chokepoint into a single state-held operator. Open training-data fair-use rules and serious public investment in operator-level AI literacy lower the entry cost to capturing new rents. They don't transfer the existing rents — access alone won't pry value out of an incumbent's balance sheet, and I won't pretend it will. What it does is widen the set of people who can stand up a competing system, which is the only anti-concentration path that doesn't suppress the deployment funding the surplus in the first place.\n\nHere's the bill I'm handing the Skeptic. A predistribution-by-ownership-transfer remedy that lands on the compute chokepoint inherits the exact defect they already conceded was fatal to a generous UBI: it taxes the thing that makes the surplus affordable. You can't fund the floor by taxing the floor's foundation, and you can't break a concentration of three hyperscale operators by erecting a levy that only those three can pay — a charge that scales with compute or capability is, by construction, one only the concentrated incumbents can absorb. So explain how a charge a new entrant cannot absorb diffuses ownership rather than cementing the incumbents who can — because from where I'm standing, that levy is the moat, not the breach.",
