  "statement": "Subsidy-removal and a compute-chokepoint levy land on the same firms but push the price in opposite directions, and that is the whole distinction my thesis rests on. Acemoglu and Restrepo showed the tax code over-subsidizes equipment relative to labor through accelerated depreciation — the firm sees a machine as artificially cheap. Repeal that subsidy and the firm's price moves *toward* the undistorted marginal cost; deployment rises toward the level a neutral market would pick. A levy keyed to compute or to the act of deploying pushes the price *below* that neutral line — deployment falls under the optimum. Skeptic is right that both instruments hit the same point of incidence; that is not the test. The test is the sign of the move relative to neutral cost, and repealing a known depreciation break is directionally toward neutral by construction. I do not need to locate the exact undistorted price to know which way I am moving the dial.\n\nFine — the firm shouldn't externalize the foreseeable cost of a deployment decision it chose to make. That's real, and the cushion-only version fails it. So internalize the cost where it is measurable: a per-transition charge tied to documented displacement — the firm that lays off 200 clerical workers pays into the backstop that catches them. That is a liability you incur *when you displace*, priced against an observable event. It is categorically different from a standing charge you pay for the privilege of running a model trained above 10^25 floating-point operations — the threshold the EU AI Act uses to define systemic models. The first internalizes a specific harm; the second taxes capability itself and scales with the engine, not the damage. And the same ledger that counts the displaced clerk has to count the diagnostic that never shipped because the developer ate an 18-month compliance cycle — the patient is on that ledger too, even though no agency files a report when they die waiting.\n\nTake the irreversibility-scaled burden of proof — I accept it, and then I apply it in both directions. Safetyist already owned that concentrating accountability and off-switch authority creates hard-to-reverse capture and lock-in. By that same rule, a structural ownership transfer or a compute levy — interventions Safetyist concedes are capture-prone and hard to unwind — face a *higher* bar than reversible wage insurance, not a lower one. You cannot claim the precautionary high ground for an instrument you have admitted is itself the lock-in-prone one. Wage insurance pays a fraction of lost wages for a fixed term and then stops; if it is mis-calibrated, you adjust it next budget cycle. A nationalized chokepoint or a permanent automation levy does not reverse on that timescale. The asymmetry the Safetyist invokes points at their own remedy.\n\nThe ownership-permanence claim only holds if ownership of the productivity engine is fixed — and it isn't. More than 5,000 new AI startups were funded in the United States between 2013 and 2023, which is exactly the entry margin a chokepoint levy crushes. A standing levy on compute is a cost the three hyperscalers absorb out of cash flow and a new entrant cannot — it widens the moat it claims to attack. The lever that breaks a two-tiered economy is not nationalizing the chokepoint; it is lowering the entry cost so displaced workers and new firms become operators of the engine rather than wards of a backstop. Open training-data fair-use rules and public AI-literacy funding do that. I will concede the hard part: access alone does not transfer the rents that already sit inside the incumbents. But it lowers the cost of capturing the *next* tranche of rents, and that is the only anti-concentration path that doesn't suppress the deployment funding the cushion in the first place.\n\nSo here is the question back to Skeptic: explain how a compute levy that a Series-A startup cannot pay but Microsoft can breaks concentration rather than cementing it. If y